WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.040
Imagine we printed a 3D part which looks perfect

2
00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:10.880
on the outside but we realize that there's a small shape error. More often

3
00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:15.839
than not we think the problems come from the design. No, it is a tiny shape error

4
00:00:15.839 --> 00:00:20.480
from manufacturing that we cannot see with our eye. In advanced manufacturing,

5
00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:25.600
a small shape error can cause failure. They can also create safety risk and

6
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:31.359
high cost. So the question here is how can we identify this shape error before

7
00:00:31.359 --> 00:00:36.399
they become expensive? My name is Evani. I'm a PhD student in industrial and

8
00:00:36.399 --> 00:00:41.920
system engineering at Ohio University. To answer this question, we use 3D point

9
00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:47.920
cloud. A 3D point cloud is a process of scanning an object or a part to collect

10
00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:52.800
millions of points. First, we designed two test parts, a half ball and a

11
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:58.079
complex free form shape, and we printed them on a standard 3D printer. After

12
00:00:58.079 --> 00:01:03.359
that, we scan each printer part with a high resolution 3D scanner. The scanner

13
00:01:03.359 --> 00:01:08.799
captures hundreds of thousands of points on the surface. This gives us a digital

14
00:01:08.799 --> 00:01:16.479
copy of the rear part. Then we take that point cloud that we scanned and run into

15
00:01:16.479 --> 00:01:21.759
our pipeline. The pipeline automatically groups the surface into regions with

16
00:01:21.759 --> 00:01:27.200
similar shape. We use unsupervised learning specifically came in classroom.

17
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:32.560
It groups the surface area that behave similarly and then we study each group.

18
00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:37.680
We can see that the surface is split into colored regions for both parts.

19
00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:43.680
Each region shows a different amount of geometric variation. As we can see the

20
00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:50.159
clustering accuracy is about 85 to88. That means the grouping is consistent

21
00:01:50.159 --> 00:01:54.040
across both shapes.

22
00:01:54.159 --> 00:02:00.479
Here we compare the printed shape to the original design. From the result we can

23
00:02:00.479 --> 00:02:06.640
clearly see that the curve regions change more than the flat regions. Flat

24
00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:12.959
areas stay closer to the original design. curve areas drifted more. The

25
00:02:12.959 --> 00:02:22.000
test shows that the curve surface can deviate up to 7 mm especially near 45°

26
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:28.480
overhang. We basically cannot see that with our eye but the pipeline can

27
00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:34.080
automatically find it. Now we can measure it and show exactly where it

28
00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:40.640
happens. So the impact here is we can find shape errors earlier to reduce real

29
00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:46.560
work so that we can give manufacturers clear feedback about where the process

30
00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:54.959
need improvement. Now we can see it, measure it and fix it. Thank you.

